---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ResourceCard from '../../../components/ResourceCard.astro';
import resources from '../../../data/resources.json';

const lang = 'en';

const relatedResources = resources.filter(r =>
  r.scienceTags.includes('siegel')
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Window of Tolerance — The Science — Activist Resiliency"
  description="Dan Siegel's window of tolerance concept — the optimal arousal zone for activists, how sustained work narrows it, and how practices widen it."
  lang={lang}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/en/science">The Science</a> &rsaquo; Window of Tolerance</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Window of Tolerance</h1>
      <p class="page-byline">Concept by Dan Siegel &bull; Applied in trauma-informed and somatic practice</p>
      <p class="page-intro">
        The window of tolerance describes the optimal zone of nervous system arousal
        where a person can function effectively — thinking clearly, feeling fully, and
        responding with flexibility. Developed by neuropsychiatrist Dan Siegel, this
        concept has become central to trauma-informed care and resilience practice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="content-article">
    <div class="container">

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Is the Window of Tolerance?</h2>
        <p>
          The window of tolerance is the zone between two extremes of arousal:
          hyper-arousal (too activated) and hypo-arousal (too shut down). Within
          the window, the brain's prefrontal cortex — responsible for reasoning,
          planning, and compassion — stays online. We can hold complexity, regulate
          our emotions, and engage with others.
        </p>
        <p>
          Outside the window, in either direction, thinking becomes impaired.
          Reactive patterns take over. The capacity for nuanced judgment narrows.
          We are operating from older parts of the brain oriented toward survival,
          not collaboration.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section diagram-section">
        <h2>The Window: A Visual Map</h2>

        <div class="tolerance-diagram" aria-label="Window of tolerance diagram showing hyper-arousal zone above, window of tolerance in the middle, and hypo-arousal zone below">
          <div class="zone zone-hyper">
            <div class="zone-label">HYPER-AROUSAL</div>
            <div class="zone-description">
              <strong>Above the window</strong><br/>
              Anxiety &bull; Rage &bull; Panic &bull; Overwhelm<br/>
              Racing thoughts &bull; Hypervigilance &bull; Reactivity<br/>
              Impulsive action &bull; Emotional flooding
            </div>
            <div class="zone-arrow zone-arrow-up">&#9650;</div>
          </div>

          <div class="zone zone-window">
            <div class="zone-label">WINDOW OF TOLERANCE</div>
            <div class="zone-description">
              <strong>Optimal arousal zone</strong><br/>
              Clear thinking &bull; Flexible response &bull; Empathy<br/>
              Capacity to be present &bull; Access to values<br/>
              Emotional regulation &bull; Grounded action
            </div>
            <div class="zone-arrows-both">
              <span class="arrow-label">&#9650; activation</span>
              <span class="arrow-label">&#9660; settling</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="zone zone-hypo">
            <div class="zone-label">HYPO-AROUSAL</div>
            <div class="zone-description">
              <strong>Below the window</strong><br/>
              Numbness &bull; Disconnection &bull; Collapse &bull; Hopelessness<br/>
              Depression &bull; Dissociation &bull; Apathy<br/>
              Inability to act &bull; Emotional flatness
            </div>
            <div class="zone-arrow zone-arrow-down">&#9660;</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How Activist Work Affects the Window</h2>
        <p>
          Activist work involves repeated exposure to material that is genuinely
          distressing: injustice, suffering, threat, confrontation, loss, and
          sometimes violence. This is not a sign that activists are fragile — it
          is a sign that they are human, and that they care.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over time, without adequate recovery and regulation, this repeated exposure
          narrows the window of tolerance. What might once have been a manageable
          level of activation — a difficult meeting, an upsetting news story, a
          setback in organizing — becomes enough to push someone out of their window
          entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          A narrowed window looks different for different people:
        </p>
        <ul class="effects-list">
          <li><strong>Hyper:</strong> Hair-trigger anger, inability to disengage from crisis news, all-or-nothing thinking, exhaustion combined with inability to stop, interpersonal conflict.</li>
          <li><strong>Hypo:</strong> Inability to feel outrage anymore, numbness to suffering, disengagement, depression, difficulty caring about outcomes that once felt urgent.</li>
          <li><strong>Oscillation:</strong> Many people cycle between the two — periods of frantic activation followed by crashes into shutdown.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How Practices Widen the Window</h2>
        <p>
          The encouraging finding from Siegel's research — and from the broader
          neuroscience of neuroplasticity — is that the window of tolerance is not
          fixed. It can be widened through consistent, intentional practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Practices that build the regulatory capacity to widen the window include:
        </p>
        <ul class="practices-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Mindfulness and present-moment awareness</strong> — builds the
            capacity to observe experience without being overwhelmed by it.
            ("I notice I am angry" rather than "I am nothing but anger.")
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Somatic practices</strong> — body-based awareness, yoga, breath
            work, and movement help complete incomplete stress cycles and build
            tolerance for physical sensation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Titration</strong> — gradually approaching distressing material
            in small amounts, with full support and recovery time, rather than
            flooding exposure.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Interpersonal co-regulation</strong> — spending time with
            people whose nervous systems are regulated helps our own systems settle.
            Social connection is a primary regulator, not a luxury.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Adequate sleep and physical recovery</strong> — the window
            narrows dramatically with sleep deprivation. This is not weakness;
            it is neurobiology.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Siegel's Wheel of Awareness</h2>
        <p>
          Dan Siegel developed a complementary practice called the Wheel of Awareness —
          a guided meditation that trains the capacity to distinguish between the
          observer (the "hub" of the wheel) and what is being observed (the "rim").
          Regular practice builds integration: the capacity to hold multiple experiences
          simultaneously without collapsing into any single one.
        </p>
        <p>
          This integration is central to resilient activist work. It is what allows
          someone to witness suffering without being consumed by it, to experience
          anger without being controlled by it, and to stay present in difficult
          conversations without shutting down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section resources-section">
        <h2>Related Resources</h2>
        <p class="resources-intro">
          The following resources draw on Dan Siegel's window of tolerance framework
          and related approaches to regulation and integration.
        </p>
        <div class="resources-grid">
          {relatedResources.map(resource => (
            <ResourceCard resource={resource} lang={lang} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-sage-light);
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .breadcrumb a {
    color: var(--color-sage);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .breadcrumb a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .page-title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .page-byline {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .page-intro {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .content-article {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .content-section {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .content-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .content-section p {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* Diagram */
  .diagram-section {
    max-width: 540px;
  }

  .tolerance-diagram {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 2px solid var(--color-sage-light);
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  .zone {
    padding: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .zone-label {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .zone-description {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
  }

  .zone-hyper {
    background-color: #fdf0ec;
    border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--color-clay);
  }

  .zone-hyper .zone-label {
    color: var(--color-clay);
  }

  .zone-window {
    background-color: #eef4ec;
    border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--color-sage);
  }

  .zone-window .zone-label {
    color: var(--color-sage);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  .zone-hypo {
    background-color: #f0eef5;
  }

  .zone-hypo .zone-label {
    color: #7a6a9a;
  }

  .zone-arrow {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .zone-arrows-both {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
  }

  .effects-list,
  .practices-list {
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0;
  }

  .effects-list li,
  .practices-list li {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
  }

  .resources-section {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .resources-section h2 {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
  }

  .resources-intro {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md) !important;
  }

  .resources-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
